## 1. Introduction & Document Purpose

ResQ AI is a short-lived, multi-agent emergency response prototype. Its documented architecture (an Emergency Orchestrator coordinating specialized agents) is significantly ahead of what has actually been implemented. The current codebase has three specific, identified structural gaps:

1. External service calls (weather, maps, LLM) do not fail gracefully — they raise unhandled exceptions that surface as raw server errors.
2. The LLM-based action-planning call produces unstructured, prose-formatted text that is parsed with regular expressions — a fragile approach with no confidence signal and duplicated severity logic.
3. There is no Orchestrator at all — the frontend independently calls multiple backend endpoints and stitches their results together client-side, which is the responsibility the Orchestrator is supposed to own.

Beyond these three structural gaps, several capability gaps exist (no discrete triage step, no confidence field anywhere in the system, no persisted SOS event log) and several scope-creep items exist (features built that were explicitly marked out-of-scope for the MVP).

This document treats every one of these as a distinct problem with its own algorithm, while being explicit about how they depend on one another, so that fixing them happens in an order that avoids rework.

