## 2. Guiding Principles Recap

Every algorithm in this document is designed to satisfy a fixed set of principles established for this project. It's worth restating them here because every design decision below traces back to one of these six rules, and understanding *why* a rule exists prevents accidentally violating it while implementing a fix.

### Principle 1 — One output, not ten
The person using this system is, by definition, in a stressful situation. They should never have to synthesize multiple disconnected pieces of information themselves. Every specialized agent's output must flow into a single coordinating layer (the Orchestrator) which produces one final, prioritized response. This principle is the reason a standalone chat assistant that bypasses the Orchestrator is a problem, even if it's technically "working" — it creates a second source of truth the user has to reconcile themselves.

### Principle 2 — Deterministic over generative for safety-critical logic
Anything that decides *how severe* a situation is, *whether* to escalate, or *whether* to trigger SOS must be governed by fixed, auditable rules — not by an LLM's token-by-token judgment, which can vary between identical inputs and cannot be fully explained after the fact. LLMs are excellent at language — phrasing, summarizing, elaborating — and should be confined to that role. This principle is why triage classification should lean on deterministic or quasi-deterministic techniques (keyword rules, embedding similarity) rather than an LLM call, and why the risk score must remain the sole authority on severity rather than letting the LLM also produce an "emergency level."

### Principle 3 — Explainability is mandatory
No score or recommendation may be a black box. If the system says "this is a Warning-level flood risk," it must also be able to say "because rainfall intensity is at 82% and drainage capacity is a fixed 54%." This principle drives the entire design of the confidence aggregation algorithm (Fix 4) — a confidence label without a reason attached is not actually explainable, it's just another opaque number.

### Principle 4 — Confidence must be visible
Anywhere the system is inferring rather than measuring — classifying an emergency type from ambiguous text, generating advice from a language model, or working with partial data because an external service failed — that uncertainty must be surfaced to the user rather than hidden behind confident-sounding prose. This is the principle most violated by the current codebase, since no confidence field exists anywhere in the current schemas.

### Principle 5 — This is decision support, not an authoritative warning system
Nothing the system says should be phrased as an official declaration. Distinguishing AI-assisted guidance from verified, live, or official data is not a cosmetic labeling exercise — it's core to preventing a user from over-trusting a heuristic risk score or an LLM-generated suggestion during a genuine emergency.

### Principle 6 — SOS requires explicit confirmation
Location sharing and emergency escalation must never be automatic. A user must take a clear, deliberate action before their location is captured and transmitted. This principle is already correctly implemented in the current codebase and must be preserved through any changes to the SOS flow (Fix 6).

