## 7. Fix 5 — Emergency Orchestrator Algorithm

### 7.1 Why this fix exists, and why it comes after Fixes 1–4 conceptually

The Orchestrator is the piece of architecture that turns a collection of independently-functioning services into the single coordinated system the project is meant to be. It cannot be meaningfully designed or built before Fixes 1 through 4 exist, because its entire function is to call those pieces and combine their outputs — if those pieces don't yet fail gracefully, don't yet produce structured output, don't yet have a discrete classification step, and don't yet have a confidence signal to aggregate, then the Orchestrator would have nothing well-formed to coordinate. Building it first would mean building it against unstable foundations and then reworking it once those foundations changed underneath it.

### 7.2 What the Orchestrator receives and what it must produce

**Input:** A single incoming emergency description from a user (text, at minimum, under the current MVP scope), optionally accompanied by location coordinates and a language preference.

**Output:** One unified response object containing: a plain-language explanation of the situation, the deterministic risk/severity level and its contributing factors, the classified emergency category, an ordered list of immediate actions and things to avoid, relevant emergency contacts, nearby resource suggestions (when location was available and the lookup succeeded), the final aggregated confidence level and its reason, and clear labeling throughout of which parts of the response came from live data, which from a database, and which from AI-generated reasoning — satisfying Principle 5's requirement to distinguish these sources explicitly rather than presenting everything with uniform authority.

### 7.3 The coordination algorithm, step by step

**Step 1 — Receive and validate the incoming request.** Confirm the minimum required input (the emergency description) is present; if location was provided, retain it for use in later steps; if a language preference was provided, retain it for the generation step.

**Step 2 — Run triage classification (Fix 3).** Pass the user's description through the tiered classifier. Receive back a category (or "unclassified"), a confidence level, and an explanation of the match. This step happens early because its output — the identified category — becomes an input to the generation step later, per the design established in Fix 2.

**Step 3 — Run the deterministic risk assessment, if location and weather data are relevant to the classified category.** This step depends on the weather service from Fix 1. Attempt to retrieve live weather data for the given location; if it succeeds, compute the deterministic risk score and retain its contributing factors; if it comes back "unavailable," retain that fact as a data-availability signal for later confidence aggregation, and decide — as a policy set by the Orchestrator, not by the weather service itself — whether to omit the risk score entirely or note that this component "couldn't be assessed live right now."

**Step 4 — Run resource lookups, if location was provided.** Attempt hospital and shelter lookups in parallel (there's no dependency between them). Each may independently succeed or come back "unavailable" per Fix 1's pattern; retain both outcomes for later confidence aggregation, and pass through whichever resource data did succeed.

**Step 5 — Run the action-planning generation step (Fix 2), providing it the classified category from Step 2 as context**, along with the user's original description, city/language, and any risk-level information gathered so far. Receive back the structured guidance object, including its self-reported confidence per Fix 2's design. If this call fails or returns non-compliant output, treat it per the fallback path described in Fix 1 and Fix 2 — proceed with a prepared static fallback message rather than blocking the entire response on this one step's failure.

**Step 6 — Aggregate confidence (Fix 4).** With the triage confidence from Step 2, the data-availability outcomes from Steps 3 and 4, and the generation self-report from Step 5 all now available, compute the final confidence level and its explanatory reason using the weakest-link algorithm.

**Step 7 — Assemble the single unified response.** Combine the classified category, the risk score and its contributing factors (or an honest note that it couldn't be computed), the generated guidance content, the resource lookup results (or an honest note that they couldn't be retrieved), the final confidence level and reason, and explicit source-tags on each major section (live data / database / AI-generated / community, per whichever applies) into one object. This is the only thing returned to the user — no individual step's raw output is exposed on its own, which is what fulfills Principle 1.

**Step 8 — Log the interaction for the emergency record**, consistent with how the current system already logs emergency assessments, so there remains an audit trail of what was classified, what was recommended, and at what confidence — independent of whether the user proceeds to trigger SOS.

### 7.4 Handling partial failure gracefully across the whole flow

A key property this algorithm must have: **no single step's failure should cause the entire response to fail**, except the total, unrecoverable failure of the generation step in Step 5 with no fallback available, which is the one truly critical path (since without it, there is no guidance to give at all). Every other step — risk scoring, resource lookup — can fail independently while the Orchestrator still produces a meaningful, honestly-labeled partial response. This is the direct payoff of doing Fix 1 first: the Orchestrator's resilience is only possible because every step it calls already knows how to fail without crashing.

### 7.5 Where this algorithm intentionally stays simple

This design deliberately does not include: parallel speculative execution beyond the two independent resource lookups in Step 4, retries with backoff, caching of prior results across requests, or a plugin-style dynamic agent-selection mechanism (the docx's broader ten-agent vision) beyond what the MVP scope requires today. These are reasonable future directions, but building them now — for a project with a lifespan of a few days — would be solving problems the project doesn't have yet, consistent with the same reasoning that ruled out the token-compression tool earlier in this project's design conversations.

### 7.6 What "done" looks like for this fix

A single incoming request, run through this coordination sequence, produces exactly one response object regardless of how many of its internal steps succeeded or failed individually; artificially forcing any one dependent service to fail (weather, maps, or the LLM) during testing still produces a coherent, honestly-labeled response rather than a crash or a silently incomplete answer.

