## 10. Deployment Considerations (Deliberately Parked)

This section exists only to record that the choice of hosting platform for the frontend and backend has been consciously deferred until the fixes above are complete, and to summarize why that sequencing is correct rather than avoidant.

**The reasoning for deferring:** Deployment-platform decisions (which service hosts the backend, how much memory it provides, whether a chosen technique like a local embedding model fits within that platform's constraints) are downstream of, and dependent on, decisions this document already covers — specifically, the embedding-model choice within Fix 3 has direct implications for memory footprint and dependency size, which in turn affects which hosting platforms are viable. Choosing a deployment platform before finalizing that technical detail risks discovering a constraint conflict after the fact, whereas finishing the technical design first and then matching it to a hosting platform avoids that risk entirely.

**What remains open when this is revisited:** A comparison of actual, current memory and deployment-size limits across the hosting platforms under consideration, matched against the actual footprint of whichever embedding approach Fix 3 ends up using, plus confirmation of how the backend framework needs to be structured differently (if at all) depending on whether the chosen platform runs a persistent process or a stateless, serverless function model — since these two hosting models have meaningfully different implications for a design (like the embedding classifier's cached-in-memory canonical examples) that assumes a long-lived, persistent process.

