## 11. Build Sequencing & Dependency Reasoning

This section exists to make the dependency relationships between every fix explicit in one place, so the reasoning behind the recommended order isn't scattered across multiple sections.

**Fix 1 (graceful failure handling) has no dependencies on any other fix**, and every other fix either directly or indirectly assumes it exists. This is why it is first.

**Fix 2 (structured JSON output) depends on Fix 1** in the sense that its own failure path (a non-compliant model response) is handled using the exact same "unavailable" pattern established in Fix 1 — building Fix 2 first would mean inventing a one-off failure-handling approach for this specific call, then having to reconcile it with Fix 1's more general pattern later.

**Fix 3 (tiered triage) has no hard dependency on Fixes 1 or 2 to be designed**, and could technically be built in parallel — but its Tier 3 fallback (the LLM classification call) benefits from the same failure-handling pattern as Fix 1, and its output is intended to feed into Fix 2's generation step as upstream context, so building it after Fix 2 exists means the integration point between them is already in place rather than needing to be retrofitted.

**Fix 4 (confidence aggregation) directly depends on Fix 1 (for the data-availability signal), Fix 2 (for the generated-content self-report signal), and Fix 3 (for the triage confidence signal)** — it has no independent existence without all three of these already producing the specific signals it aggregates. This is why it cannot be built, or even fully specified in implementation terms, before those three exist.

**Fix 5 (the Orchestrator) depends on all four preceding fixes simultaneously** — it is, by design, the layer that calls Fix 1's resilient services, invokes Fix 3's classifier, calls Fix 2's structured generation step, and performs Fix 4's aggregation. It is intentionally the last of the core five fixes for this reason; building it earlier would mean building a coordination layer with nothing stable yet to coordinate.

**Fix 6 (SOS event logging) is independent of Fixes 1 through 5 entirely** — it touches a different part of the system (the SOS trigger flow) that doesn't share code paths with the assessment/triage/generation flow. It can be built at any point in this sequence, including in parallel with any of the other fixes, without dependency conflicts. It is listed last in this document only because it was the most recently discussed, not because it must come last in implementation.

**The scope governance decisions (Section 9) should ideally be resolved before or during Fix 5's construction**, specifically because two of the three decisions (community reports, the standalone chat assistant) directly change what Fix 5 needs to be designed to handle. Resolving them after Fix 5 is built risks rework; resolving them before or during means Fix 5 is built once, correctly scoped from the start.

**Deployment (Section 10) correctly remains last**, dependent on the technical choices made throughout, particularly within Fix 3.

